          "field": "description",
          "page_id": "page-f0ed83931b81f17677e28a22",
          "qa_findings": [],
          "review_status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "segment_id": "seg-2cef095680555a1a5afa7a81",
          "segment_index": 13,
          "source_segment_id": "seg-2cef095680555a1a5afa7a81",
          "source_text_sha256": "91d1496a4aa1d6417438114b85b1c65959de589d1ed133f0359bb6aa406d9d6c",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: On June 30, 2015, between 11:30 PM and midnight, a witness observed for an extended period two unusual lights in the sky, colored yellow-orange, positioned one above the other. He took several photos. The observation of slowly moving bright points immediately suggests an astronomical origin. A check on the star map reveals that on that evening, Venus and Jupiter were visible at their closest, just above the horizon O/NO: this rare phenomenon of apparent proximity is called a conjunction. The attached star maps show the appearance of the phenomenon at 11:33 PM, and again at 11:55 PM when the planets were about to disappear below the horizon. The GEIPAN classifies this case as \"A,\" perfectly explained: conjunction of Venus and Jupiter.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "6d6901ec593dea5ab2501872ed34bd21ed2501274e5c09f4aa971924db860600"
        }
